From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Alexander=20Thei=C3=9Fen?= <alex@theissen.io>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:28:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/15] wined3d: Fix table fog with equal start and end and an
 Apple GL buffer texture crash.

Table fog with FOGSTART equal to FOGEND leaves coordinates below the
start unfogged and fogs those above it, but the fog scale passed to the
shader was 1 / (end - start), which is infinite. A coordinate equal to
the end gave 0 * inf = NaN on every driver. Drivers that evaluate
(end - coord) * scale per vertex, as KosmicKrisp's NIR varying
optimisation does with the shaders Zink gives it, interpolate the
infinities of opposite signs at the vertices to NaN, and the whole
primitive stayed unfogged. The scale is now finite and makes the step
about one ULP of the end wide, which also keeps the product finite for
a huge end such as FLT_MAX, so the result no longer depends on how a
driver handles infinities. The change applies to every adapter, not
only to Zink on KosmicKrisp. Drivers that evaluate the fog factor per
pixel give the same result as before outside that step. The checks of
the unfogged side in the d3d8, d3d9 and ddraw7 fog special tests have
been todo_wine since Wine's CI moved to Debian trixie; they succeed on
Zink and on Apple's OpenGL and are no longer todo.
On Zink, the d3d9 test_fog checks of the FFP's table fog with an
orthogonal projection and the two test_texture_transform_flags checks
of a projected D3DTTFF_COUNT1 coordinate succeed as well. The latter
also succeed on Apple's OpenGL and are no longer todo; the fog checks
still fail there, where wined3d reports a GeForce 6800 (device id
0x0041), a Direct3D 9 class card, and stay todo_wine for that adapter.

Keep the buffer of the dummy buffer texture until the texture is
deleted. Deleting its name right after glTexBuffer() is valid GL, but
once Apple's OpenGL reuses that name for another buffer, glBufferData()
on a later GL_TEXTURE_BUFFER buffer faults inside GLEngine. The fault
leaves the context's lock held, and the next GL call on that thread
aborts the process. Buffers created with shader resource or unordered
access bind flags use that binding.

(cherry picked from commit 158b218383d0b448c23aec28a8651485297070c9)
